.TH SECSTORED 8
.SH NAME
secstored \- secure store server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/secstored
.B -d
.I storedir
[
.B -a
.I announce
] [
.B -n
.I name
]
.br
.B auth/secstored
.B -d
.I storedir
.B -c
.I user
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Secstored
is the server for
.IR secstore (1):
it holds each user's files and serves them over a
.IR secstore (2)
connection \(em a PAK-authenticated, encrypted channel in which the
server never learns the user's password, only a verifier.
The tree previously shipped only the client; this is the matching server,
so you can run your own secstore and so
.IR factotum (4)
.B -S
has something to fetch from.
.PP
Storage lives under
.IR storedir :
each user has a directory
.IB storedir / user
holding their files, and a verifier
.IB storedir / user /.pak.
Files are stored exactly as the client uploads them \(em already
encrypted client-side \(em so the server holds no plaintext.
.SS Options
.TP
.B -d
.I storedir
The store directory (required).
.TP
.B -a
.I announce
The address to serve, default
.BR tcp!*!secstore .
.TP
.B -n
.I name
The server name reported to clients, default
.BR secstore .
.TP
.B -c
.I user
Create an account: prompt for a new password and write its verifier,
then exit. Do this once per user before serving.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX

	auth/secstored -d /adm/secstore -c ann
	auth/secstored -d /adm/secstore -a 'tcp!*!5356' &

.EE
Then, from a client, seed factotum from it:
.EX

	auth/factotum -S ann@tcp!server!5356

.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/secstored.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR secstore (1),
.IR secstore (2),
.IR factotum (4)
.SH BUGS
No third-party-caveat, STA/SecurID, or account-removal support.
